The event-delivery substrate in com.intempt.core.queue is adapted from the
Mixpanel Android SDK. The following files are derivative works:

  EventDbAdapter.java        from mpmetrics/MPDbAdapter.java
  DeliveryMessages.java      from mpmetrics/AnalyticsMessages.java
  HttpService.java           from util/HttpService.java
  RemoteService.java         from util/RemoteService.java
  SynchronizedReference.java from mpmetrics/SynchronizedReference.java
  NetworkErrorListener.java  from util/MixpanelNetworkErrorListener.java
  OfflineMode.java           from util/OfflineMode.java
  ProxyServerInteractor.java from util/ProxyServerInteractor.java

Each of those files carries a header recording its specific modifications, as
required by section 4(b) of the License. In summary:

  - package and class names changed to com.intempt.core.queue
  - the four-table schema (events, people, groups, anonymous_people) reduced to
    a single events table
  - the multi-token column and the static multi-instance registry removed;
    Intempt runs one SDK instance per application
  - people, group, anonymous-profile and first-launch message types removed
  - Mixpanel's default event properties and the session-replay event bridge
    removed
  - form-urlencoded transport encoding replaced with Intempt's JSON contract
  - send-once delivery replaces the HTTP error taxonomy, the in-call retry loop
    and the exponential backoff: a 503 or 429 is resent once after its
    Retry-After, every other failure drops the batch, and only a batch that
    never reached the server is kept for the next flush
  - delivery failures are reported once per batch, not once per attempt

The following behaviour is inherited unchanged, and deliberately so:
delete-only-after-confirmed-delivery, the flush-suppression guard while a
never-sent batch is kept, the
disk-space backpressure calculation, the per-row malformed-record skip, the
batch size limit, SQLite corruption recovery on writes and its deliberate
absence on reads, the created_at index, the data-expiration sweep, the
single-writer worker thread, and the connectivity gate before posting.

